WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.969 --> 00:00:04.130
I’ve got a $20 bill here, I can buy stuff!

00:00:04.130 --> 00:00:05.750
But, wait.

00:00:05.750 --> 00:00:07.700
This is just a piece of paper.

00:00:07.700 --> 00:00:12.420
Who says anyone will trust that this is worth $20?

00:00:12.420 --> 00:00:16.320
Money is only useful if at least two people

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:18.640
agree that it can be trusted.

00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:20.160
A new documentary asks,

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:23.570
what makes money trustworthy?

00:00:23.570 --> 00:00:27.720
It has to be reliable, like a clock, says Steve Forbes.

00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:29.800
It has to be fixed in value.

00:00:29.810 --> 00:00:30.760
Sixty minutes in an hour;

00:00:30.760 --> 00:00:32.280
sixty seconds in a minute.

00:00:32.280 --> 00:00:34.400
Imagine if that floated each day,

00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:36.720
that would make life chaotic.

00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:37.700
So throughout history,

00:00:37.700 --> 00:00:41.460
people needed a way to assign a fixed value to money.

00:00:41.460 --> 00:00:43.780
The best mechanism for this would be some

00:00:43.780 --> 00:00:45.960
kind of commodity that’s permanent,

00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:50.080
easily transported, easily understood by everyone.

00:00:50.080 --> 00:00:52.490
And that medium was, of course, gold.

00:00:52.490 --> 00:00:54.580
Not “of course,” people have pegged money

00:00:54.580 --> 00:00:56.920
to agricultural crops, or salt,

00:00:56.920 --> 00:00:59.060
that's where we got the word “salary,”

00:00:59.060 --> 00:01:02.320
but gold was more reliable.

00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:04.180
And once it was accepted as currency

00:01:04.180 --> 00:01:06.330
Money begins to create a kind of mobility

00:01:06.330 --> 00:01:09.170
in people’s lives that they never had before.

00:01:09.170 --> 00:01:10.170
But gold is heavy!

00:01:10.170 --> 00:01:12.060
It’s hard to carry around.

00:01:12.060 --> 00:01:13.830
That limited trade.

00:01:13.830 --> 00:01:15.460
So people invented banks.

00:01:15.460 --> 00:01:17.100
The Knights Templar developed a system where

00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:18.500
they said, well, you can just deposit your

00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:21.040
money here with us and then, when you need some,

00:01:21.040 --> 00:01:23.010
you can withdraw it from your account.

00:01:23.010 --> 00:01:25.060
This enabled the peasants to travel Europe

00:01:25.060 --> 00:01:27.540
without being a danger of being robbed.

00:01:27.540 --> 00:01:30.260
That enabled much more trade.

00:01:30.260 --> 00:01:31.860
You could sell a bond in London,

00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:34.380
and build a railroad in India.

00:01:34.380 --> 00:01:35.920
And, as a result,

00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:38.520
this seemingly resource poor

00:01:38.520 --> 00:01:41.909
speck of Europe became the financial

00:01:41.909 --> 00:01:43.140
center of the world.

00:01:43.140 --> 00:01:46.320
Then, in the US Alexander Hamilton fixed

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:47.869
the dollar to gold.

00:01:47.869 --> 00:01:51.360
The dollar became to be seen as a rock solid currency

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:53.320
where you could go anywhere in the world

00:01:53.320 --> 00:01:54.960
and they would accept that dollar.

00:01:54.960 --> 00:01:58.080
and the world boomed.

00:01:58.080 --> 00:01:59.150
Wages soared.

00:01:59.150 --> 00:02:01.070
It was an incredible period of wealth creation.

00:02:01.070 --> 00:02:02.800
Don’t get me wrong, there were examples

00:02:02.800 --> 00:02:05.100
of terrible deprivation and hunger.

00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:07.939
But it was a time of great material progress.

00:02:07.939 --> 00:02:11.020
And the monetary foundation of that progress

00:02:11.020 --> 00:02:12.760
lay in the Gold Standard.

00:02:12.760 --> 00:02:16.111
But as usual, rulers would debase the currency,

00:02:16.111 --> 00:02:17.180
The Emperor Nero

00:02:17.180 --> 00:02:19.000
They would call in all the coins,

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:21.200
melt them down, reissue them,

00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:23.060
of course, with his picture on them.

00:02:23.060 --> 00:02:24.090
But with less gold

00:02:24.090 --> 00:02:27.520
Rome’s decline was very closely associated

00:02:27.520 --> 00:02:29.180
with the decline of its money.

00:02:29.180 --> 00:02:30.800
When you change the value of money,

00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:32.400
you’re stealing property

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:34.880
That happened to Germany after WWI

00:02:34.880 --> 00:02:37.700
To print money to pay for the conflict,

00:02:37.700 --> 00:02:40.209
Europe and the U.S. went off gold.

00:02:40.209 --> 00:02:41.660
And after losing the war,

00:02:41.660 --> 00:02:45.520
Germany suffered the infamous Weimar hyperinflation.

00:02:45.520 --> 00:02:46.540
We said to Germany,

00:02:46.540 --> 00:02:48.340
“You have to pay back the world.”

00:02:48.340 --> 00:02:51.380
The nation’s government certainly couldn’t

00:02:51.380 --> 00:02:52.870
pay that much money.

00:02:52.870 --> 00:02:55.970
So, Germany began to print and printed too much.

00:02:55.970 --> 00:02:58.720
You go to a restaurant and you find that

00:02:58.720 --> 00:03:01.710
your meal is going to cost eight thousand marks,

00:03:01.710 --> 00:03:03.580
say, and you order it, you eat it,

00:03:03.580 --> 00:03:05.740
and by the time the bill comes,

00:03:05.740 --> 00:03:07.580
that eight thousand Marks

00:03:07.580 --> 00:03:09.920
becomes sixteen thousand Marks.

00:03:09.920 --> 00:03:11.840
In the United States, around that time,

00:03:11.840 --> 00:03:14.950
politicians created our own crisis.

00:03:14.950 --> 00:03:17.300
President Herbert Hoover signed into law

00:03:17.300 --> 00:03:19.290
the Smoot Hawley Tariff.

00:03:19.290 --> 00:03:22.220
Its sweeping taxes on thousands of foreign products

00:03:22.220 --> 00:03:24.700
triggered a global trade war.

00:03:24.700 --> 00:03:26.600
This set in motion events leading

00:03:26.600 --> 00:03:28.349
to the Great Depression.

00:03:28.349 --> 00:03:29.720
With millions unemployed,

00:03:29.720 --> 00:03:32.050
governments searched for solutions.

00:03:32.050 --> 00:03:33.700
They tightened the money

00:03:33.700 --> 00:03:36.970
and the country literally ran out of money.

00:03:36.970 --> 00:03:39.790
So, in some towns, there was no money left.

00:03:39.790 --> 00:03:40.790
The bank closed.

00:03:40.790 --> 00:03:46.160
I am going to establish a government market for gold.

00:03:46.160 --> 00:03:47.300
Franklin Roosevelt,

00:03:47.300 --> 00:03:50.480
in an attempt to revive a struggling US economy,

00:03:50.480 --> 00:03:53.260
confiscated private holdings of gold

00:03:53.260 --> 00:03:55.140
and devalued the dollar.

00:03:55.150 --> 00:03:58.120
So, all kinds of constitutional freedoms

00:03:58.120 --> 00:04:01.819
and this moral commitment to maintain the value

00:04:01.819 --> 00:04:03.580
of the money were swept out

00:04:03.580 --> 00:04:05.430
Roosevelt really didn’t know what to do.

00:04:05.430 --> 00:04:06.740
so he kind of tried every theory,

00:04:06.740 --> 00:04:08.460
the way people will do,

00:04:08.460 --> 00:04:09.690
like a cook in a kitchen,

00:04:09.690 --> 00:04:11.050
trying different spices.

00:04:11.050 --> 00:04:12.360
Near the end of World War II,

00:04:12.360 --> 00:04:16.940
Western governments met to try to stabilize currencies.

00:04:16.940 --> 00:04:20.120
The world returned to gold-based money.

00:04:20.120 --> 00:04:21.489
And it was enormously successful.

00:04:21.489 --> 00:04:24.340
Those two decades, from 1950 to 1960,

00:04:24.340 --> 00:04:28.260
were the most successful economically of any time.

00:04:28.260 --> 00:04:29.380
But then, again,

00:04:29.380 --> 00:04:31.860
a President abandoned the Gold Standard.

00:04:31.860 --> 00:04:33.720
I directed Secretary Connelly

00:04:33.720 --> 00:04:36.860
to suspend temporarily the convertibility of the dollar.

00:04:36.860 --> 00:04:38.540
The value of the dollar plunged.

00:04:38.540 --> 00:04:40.460
Since then the dollar lost

00:04:40.460 --> 00:04:43.010
more than 80% of its value.

00:04:43.010 --> 00:04:44.800
That’s one reason that today

00:04:44.800 --> 00:04:47.180
Some believe the future lies in

00:04:47.180 --> 00:04:49.800
cryptocurrencies like Bitcoin.

00:04:49.800 --> 00:04:52.580
Bitcoin is both a currency and a payment system,

00:04:52.580 --> 00:04:56.720
so it’s like PayPal and U.S. dollars combined into one.

00:04:56.720 --> 00:04:59.220
But so far, crypto-currencies’s price changes

00:04:59.220 --> 00:05:01.780
make them hard to use as currency.

00:05:01.780 --> 00:05:03.610
Can we transact with Bitcoin today?

00:05:03.610 --> 00:05:05.440
Oh, I’ll pay you twenty Bitcoin

00:05:05.440 --> 00:05:07.169
if you come to work for me.

00:05:07.169 --> 00:05:08.169
That will be your annual salary.

00:05:08.169 --> 00:05:10.300
Was it the Bitcoin that was worth 980

00:05:10.300 --> 00:05:11.850
at the beginning of 2017?

00:05:11.850 --> 00:05:16.350
Or, the Bitcoin that was worth 22,000 when 2018 began?

00:05:16.350 --> 00:05:18.140
So what does the future hold?

00:05:18.140 --> 00:05:20.480
Here’s the documentary’s concluding argument:

00:05:20.480 --> 00:05:23.700
The only way to restore trust is for the United States

00:05:23.700 --> 00:05:25.300
to return to the system

00:05:25.300 --> 00:05:27.320
that worked for most of its history.

00:05:27.320 --> 00:05:30.820
Gold has proven to be the preferred monetary standard.

00:05:30.830 --> 00:05:34.370
These days, most economists oppose a gold standard.

00:05:34.370 --> 00:05:36.350
Milton Friedman opposed it.

00:05:36.350 --> 00:05:38.360
But this documentary will give you background

00:05:38.360 --> 00:05:42.540
to help you decide what you think.

00:05:42.540 --> 00:05:46.580
After all, we do need sound money with a stable value.

00:05:46.580 --> 00:05:50.010
All this is the struggle for trust.

00:05:50.010 --> 00:05:53.100
And money, ultimately, does depend on trust.

00:05:53.100 --> 00:05:55.280
You can see the full documentary

00:05:55.280 --> 00:05:56.760
on some PBS stations,

00:05:56.760 --> 00:06:00.249
or stream it at: InMoneyWeTrust.org.

